// W17 GATE — the `-> RetTy` return annotation PINS (SYNTAX.md «Return type
// omission»: absent = inferred; PRESENT = a CONSTRAINT unified against the
// inferred body return). The wheel now mints NTypeAnn(ty) at parse
// (parse_type_expr) carrying the REAL parsed Ty — never the old dummy LitUnit
// that discarded it — and infer_fn reads it back live and unifies the authored
// Ty against the inferred return (E_TypeMismatch with a Reason on a violation).
//
// This gate proves the PASS-THROUGH path end-to-end through the seed: every
// function below carries a return annotation that AGREES with its inferred body
// type, so the pin unifies cleanly and the program computes its result. The
// annotation present-and-agreeing is the common case the medium must compile
// byte-identically to the un-annotated form (the constraint is satisfied, no
// diagnostic). The VIOLATION path (a body whose inferred type contradicts the
// annotation -> E_TypeMismatch) is a compile-time diagnostic, not a runtime
// exit, so it is exercised by the wheel's own infer over a mismatched source,
// not assertable as a micro exit; this gate locks the agreeing path which is
// where a regression (the pin firing a spurious mismatch) would surface.
//
//   add(a, b) -> Int  with body a + b : Int   -> pin agrees
//   pick(c)   -> Int  with body (if c>0 {c*2} else {0}) : Int -> pin agrees
//   main sums the annotated calls; exit 42.

fn add(a, b) -> Int = a + b

fn pick(c) -> Int =
  if c > 0 { c * 2 } else { 0 }

fn triple(x) -> Int = x + x + x

fn main(q) = {
  let s0 = add(10, 5)      // 15
  let s1 = pick(6)         // 12
  let s2 = triple(5)       // 15
  s0 + s1 + s2             // 42
}
